WEBVTT

1
00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:32.100
"I have sinus stuck." What a common complaint we see.

2
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.945
Underlying this, sometimes we have to look at why they keep presenting with the

3
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.060
same flare-ups. So the acute episodes are what often brings them in to see the GP.

4
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:46.830
What we need to nut out is underneath these acute presentations, is there an

5
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.860
underlying feature like chronic, uncontrolled, under-medicated allergic rhinitis?

6
00:00:53.654 --> 00:00:58.470
So when you have allergic rhinitis and there's another insult, such as a viral

7
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.180
infection or a high allergen load like as in pollen season, the already

8
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:07.950
unfunctional nose tips over and creates the classic symptoms of facial pain and

9
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:13.051
pressure, increased discharge, leading to foggy brain, and feeling tired and

10
00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:18.990
lethargic. So the key here is that in between these episodes of the facial

11
00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.475
pain resolving eventually and the congestion getting a little bit better, we have

12
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.980
the underlying allergic rhinitis.

13
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:31.711
Sneezing, runny nose, occasional congestion, sometimes predominantly overnight,

14
00:01:31.801 --> 00:01:34.336
and ocular symptoms of itchy, watery eyes.

15
00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.685
And when you can separate these acute episodes and then have the underlying

16
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.990
features of allergic rhinitis underneath, you have a better diagnosis of this

17
00:01:43.051 --> 00:01:47.580
uncontrolled or under-medicated allergic rhinitis, which is tipping them over into

18
00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:51.870
these episodes of acute sinusitis or infective sinusitis.

19
00:02:01.351 --> 00:02:06.391
So really importantly in the short term, we do need to decrease his discomfort and

20
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:11.641
get him out of this acute episode. So we do aim to try to avoid antibiotics in what

21
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.095
most likely is a viral infection or exacerbation in most cases.

22
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:21.660
So we can start by adding in a topical decongestant if they haven't already started

23
00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:27.090
one. The topical decongestant will cause vasoconstriction, thereby decreasing the

24
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.770
amount of inflammation. So after the decongestant works, we can then add in an

25
00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:36.570
intranasal saline rinse. Now, the rinsing with saline will improve the clearance

26
00:02:36.601 --> 00:02:40.950
and allow the nasal mucosa to do its job, which is get rid of the virus, get rid of

27
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.391
the thick secretions, and get the nose back to its regular nasal function.

28
00:02:45.421 --> 00:02:50.235
Now, importantly, the third step is adding in the intranasal corticosteroid and

29
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:55.035
antihistamine preparation. Now, this not only will help with the decreasing of

30
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.835
acute inflammation but also the preventative effect of the underlying allergic

31
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:05.430
rhinitis. Now, really importantly, once we get this patient through this acute

32
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:11.430
episode is the stress of continuing the intranasal saline

33
00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:16.351
to help the nasal function rinse out the day-to-day allergens and pollutants and

34
00:03:16.469 --> 00:03:21.570
then keep going with the intranasal corticosteroid and antihistamine preparation

35
00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.880
for ongoing preventative maintenance.

36
00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.265
I'd really like to stress the importance of the healthcare professional making sure

37
00:03:29.281 --> 00:03:35.115
the patient understands the safety of using intranasal saline alongside

38
00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:40.410
their intranasal glucocorticoid and antihistamine topical preparation for long-term

39
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:46.140
use. It is safe, it is well documented and evidence-based, and it is

40
00:03:46.201 --> 00:03:51.105
definitely meant to stress to the patient that it is a long-term management plan,

41
00:03:51.421 --> 00:03:53.281
especially for those perennial sufferers.
